WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.290
RUXANDA DANETIU: Thank you
so much for joining us today.

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:10.500
We're going to be talking
about Kubernetes engine

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.840
infrastructure and
service oriented

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.840
monitoring with Equifax.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.310
My name is Ruxanda.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.430
I'm a product manager
on Google Cloud focused

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:25.240
on cloud monitoring, and today,
my guest is going to be Vipul.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.520
He is a lead SRE at Equifax.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.510
For the next 30
minutes, we're going

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.210
to be focusing on
three main topics.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:37.100
We're going to start by
discussing SLO monitoring

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.670
principles.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.490
We're going to do
a Q&amp;A with Vipul

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.590
from Equifax to
really understand

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:49.310
how Equifax has adopted SLOs
and the digital transformation

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:51.750
journey that they
are undertaking.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.480
And then at the end, we're
going to have a demo.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.660
Patrick, which is our
engineering lead on cloud

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.450
monitoring, is going to
give us a demo of how

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:06.240
we can use SLOs and SLO
monitoring to monitor our GKE

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.290
fleet of microservice.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.420
So let's start
thinking a little bit

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:16.350
about what are the principles
behind SLO monitoring?

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:20.160
I know probably many of
you have heard about SLOs,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.340
and when we think
about SLOs, It feels

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.170
like we are talking a lot about
numbers and targets and goals.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.440
We talk a lot about how
many 9s that we have

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.830
and how important it is to
collect these numbers of 9s,

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:37.050
almost like badges
of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.500
We talk about how important
it is for us to have the best

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.580
reliability out there,
and a lot of people

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:50.180
really try to strive extensively
to reach that 100% reliability

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:55.240
goal for basically every single
microservice that they own.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.320
And that's what I
want us to start with,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.890
from the point of realizing
that this conversation is not

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.460
about numbers.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.870
It's not about getting
every single microservice

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:10.740
to its maximum
availability possible.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:15.940
And it's 100%-- it's the
wrong target to begin with.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.610
And surprisingly
enough, even though this

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:23.890
is such an incredible
engineering driven experience,

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.020
what we were going
to talk about today

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:31.600
is actually language, because
what is more important for us

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.880
than anything else
is to figure out

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:37.690
what is a common
language that we can give

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.870
every single person
in our organization

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:45.500
the ability to communicate
towards the same success goal.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.490
So let's think about
the business process.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.950
You start with a concept.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:54.420
You end up building requirements
with your product team,

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.020
your marketing team,
your sales teams.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.250
You end up defining the
goal and the business

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:02.140
goal for your product.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.230
You start engaging with
your engineering teams

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.110
and build a development
of that product.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:12.130
You start working and engaging
around your operations teams,

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:15.430
and your operations teams wants
to ensure that what they build,

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:18.760
it's reliable and scalable,
and it's performant.

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.130
And then you start taking
the product to market.

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:25.180
The problem is, the way
that I told this story,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:29.770
is that it feels like this
is set of sequence steps,

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:32.650
and you have to introduce
all of these people

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:35.850
into the process one by one.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.750
But what is important for
us is that we actually

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.330
need to have the business, the
development, and the operations

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.670
team to collaborate
together on defining

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:51.690
what does success look like for
the organization as a whole.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.600
And when we talk
about success, it

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.200
has to be a single metric
that are all of these teams

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.050
and all of these
people get behind.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:04.700
These metrics really need to
reflect what the end customer

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:06.153
experience is all about.

00:04:06.153 --> 00:04:07.820
We need to think
about the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.670
how the end user
uses our product.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:13.190
And we need to ensure
that we are building

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:16.550
the right measurement
of that [INAUDIBLE],,

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:20.720
and we make this measurement
understandable and available

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.610
to everyone in the organization.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.810
So when we talk about
defining success,

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:31.820
SLOs gives us the ability
to create a common language

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:35.120
across business,
development, and operations

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.660
to understand what is
important for the organization

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:43.660
and how to instrument this
into trackable metrics.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.590
Because this is a
common language,

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:47.960
and it's a new language
across the org,

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.180
we're going to have
a few new terms we're

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.700
going to need to get
familiarized with,

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.580
so these are the
three core terms

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.100
of service oriented monitoring.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.550
So what is an SLI?

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:03.530
An SLI is a Service
Level Indicator.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.050
Think about this as a
metric under a threshold.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:13.480
It is a measure of what
success looks or feels like.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:17.630
An SLO is a Service
Level Objective.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:21.180
And the difference there,
think about the metric

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.830
that you build your SLI on,
and you attach a goal to it.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:28.370
So you have a fraction of
your successful interactions

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.180
that you're interested in.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.870
Let's say you have
a retail website,

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:37.370
and you care about
measuring customer happiness

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.020
with your checkout service.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:44.120
You know that a lot
of your financial team

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.010
is really interesting
to make sure

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:49.430
that people can check
out and pay for the items

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:52.160
that they care about, so we
know that is a cornerstone

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:53.640
of the experience.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.330
We know that we want the
service to be available,

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.940
but most importantly, we
don't want our customers

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:00.540
to be frustrated.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:04.250
So here, we're going to start
thinking about how fast do we

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:08.120
want our users to
experience this page,

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.100
and it could be a
matter of seconds.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:15.440
So we need to know
what share of customers

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.630
should experience
this, so maybe we

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:22.490
have a target where we can have
a business goal that at least

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:28.430
95% of all of our customers
have to open the checkout cart

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:32.480
service and visualize the data
and the items in [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.190
cart in less than five
seconds, and that is the goal.

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:40.130
So as you can notice we're
not talking about 9s.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:43.050
We are talking about the
end customer experience,

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.800
and that's a really
important clarification

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:50.090
to make as we are thinking about
how to measure the end customer

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:52.130
experience.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.740
And the last item here,
probably all of you

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:58.580
are already familiar with,
because an SLAs is a Service

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:03.440
Level Agreement, and that
takes the SLI, the metric,

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.450
attaches the goal,
but then it has

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:07.620
some consequences around it.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.740
So for example,
this is a contract

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:12.850
that you make with your
external customers.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:15.290
When you tell them that
we have an availability,

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:19.490
our product will be available
for this amount of time

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:21.830
under these
constraints, and if we

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:26.870
don't meet those requirements,
then we will offer you refunds.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:29.380
So a lot of companies
have implemented

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:33.500
SLAs in these contracts with
their external customers.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:36.650
What we are trying to
get them to understand

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.350
is we already have
the underlying.

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:42.570
Under the SLA, there
is an SLO itself,

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:45.440
and these are really
powerful metrics

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:48.200
that can transform
your organization

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:51.140
and bring your business,
your development,

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:55.680
and your operations into
a single frame of mind.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.610
So now that we have
this new language,

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.830
I think it's important
for us to see

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:04.370
what is a great example of
how to take this language

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.330
and put it into practice.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:09.350
And Equifax has been
an incredible advocate

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:12.290
within their own company
of adopting SLOs,

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:15.380
and I am extremely happy
and privileged to have,

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:18.000
today, Vipul join us.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.620
He is an SRE lead
at Equifax, and he's

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.770
going to tell us a
little bit about the SLO

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:25.900
journey [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:28.880
So, Vipul, please
introduce yourself.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:30.920
Tell us a little
bit about your role,

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:34.880
and tell us a little bit about
the Equifax architecture.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.080
VIPUL MAPARA: Thanks,
Ruxanda, for having me.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.360
It's been a pleasure.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:43.640
So about my role, I've
been with Equifax 14 years.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.410
Equifax has been great.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:49.880
I've been started as a
developer 14 years back, served

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:52.490
in various roles, and
then most recently, I've

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:57.230
been working as a SRE for one
of the Alliance data fabric

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:58.400
within Equifax.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:00.440
A little bit about Equifax--

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.070
Equifax is a global company.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.730
We are data analytics
and technology company.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:08.660
We operate into 24
countries, and the platform

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:12.320
that I'm responsible, my
day to day role as an SRE,

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:14.510
is a enterprise
data lake pattern.

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.170
And Equifax is on this digital
transformation journey,

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:21.950
and Google is our platform
of choice to enable us

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:24.300
for the transformation journey.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:28.850
So as part of that journey,
I'm responsible to implement

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:30.290
the enterprise
data lake pattern.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:32.900
Primarily, the
capabilities are in just

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:37.760
store, process, analyze, and
server over analytics API.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:43.100
So probably most of our services
that we leverage from Google

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:45.800
are Kubernetes data flow.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:48.770
Google helps us to improve
our security posture.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:52.760
I mean, it's a security first
mindset we have at Equifax,

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.260
so Google helps us on that.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:55.910
And then on operating
tools, we are

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:57.590
using Cloud Logging
and Monitoring

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:01.790
to give us the visibility into
the system set we are running.

00:10:01.790 --> 00:10:04.580
So probably that's,
in nutshell, what

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:07.220
we do, and the services and
the capabilities that we

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.080
are serving are
pretty much following

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:11.900
the container as the abstraction
and microservice architecture

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:14.280
[INAUDIBLE].

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:16.710
RUXANDA DANETIU: Vipul, you
mentioned microservices.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:19.020
You know that's going
to be my curiosity.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:21.600
Please tell me how do
you monitor the health

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:23.372
of your microservices?

00:10:23.372 --> 00:10:24.580
VIPUL MAPARA: Great question.

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:30.030
So at the beginning, we were
working off our on-prem data

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:33.690
center, and we didn't have
contain orchestration platform

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:36.190
as part of the infrastructure.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:39.510
So it was a little hard
to get a visibility

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:44.460
into our applications, and
operations was a little bit

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:45.140
tough.

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:49.080
And also, in our precloud
public cloud [INAUDIBLE],,

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:52.030
our engineering and operations
were working in silos.

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:55.530
So our first approach was, let's
lift and shift our application,

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:59.880
and then progressively moved
to a microservice architecture.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.310
But now that we have
decided to embrace

00:11:02.310 --> 00:11:05.140
our digital transformation
with the public cloud,

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:06.890
we are not just
doing lift and shift.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:10.440
We are really thinking as
rearchitecting the application

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.570
and using the
BetterCloud native tools.

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:15.485
The tools that are what
we had in data center

00:11:15.485 --> 00:11:19.140
are no more working out
for us, and we really

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:22.440
need a tool which gives us
good observabilitiy into when

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:24.610
we are moving our
applications and workloads

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:26.020
into public cloud.

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:28.380
So if you don't
know, I mean, what

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:30.930
we have to observe
because there's too much

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.980
newness about the public cloud.

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:34.770
So what you end up
is you can start

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.000
with [INAUDIBLE] on signals.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.130
I mean, as part of
SRE best practice,

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.470
to take [INAUDIBLE]
signals and then

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:42.270
work your way towards SLOs.

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:47.022
I mean, I feel that's
a good starting point.

00:11:47.022 --> 00:11:48.730
RUXANDA DANETIU: You
mentioned the cloud.

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.450
Tell me more a little bit
about the journey behind why

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:54.170
you adopted SLOs,
and what do you think

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:59.023
are the main benefits for
Equifax to start using SLOs?

00:11:59.023 --> 00:12:00.440
VIPUL MAPARA: I
mean, SLO a little

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:03.090
has been a great concept
and one of the best practice

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:06.210
that I learned, and
it's a single language

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:09.430
across our engineering team,
product team, marketing,

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:10.710
finance, sales.

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:14.580
It helps us to talk
with a common goal,

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:17.130
so it helps us to
prioritize our effort.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:21.510
And once we have that
kind of single language

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:25.690
to work across the
organization, it's much better.

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:27.900
Otherwise, you have
a lot of meetings

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:29.920
with analysis paralysis.

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:33.160
You go through a churn, but you
really don't have a direction.

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:37.990
So to give a opinionated meeting
a direction, SLO helps it,

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:41.010
and it really becomes
a data driven decision

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:43.890
that allows us to figure out
where we should prioritize

00:12:43.890 --> 00:12:45.660
our efforts and investment.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.530
And also, for SRE,
it is very important.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:51.770
You know, using SLO as
your guiding principle,

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:53.797
when to wake up and
when to not, and what

00:12:53.797 --> 00:12:55.380
really is impacting
the revenue, which

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:56.970
is important for business.

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:03.000
So I feel that's a common
theme that within Equifax

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.640
to embrace SLO as
a single language.

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:09.380
RUXANDA DANETIU: It sounds like
there are a lot of benefits.

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:11.760
But be honest with me--
what was the hardest part?

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:14.510
Because this seems
too good to be true.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:17.850
VIPUL MAPARA: True,
SLO's concept great,

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:22.410
but to put it in execution is
definitely, definitely hard.

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:26.820
The way I articulate
the SLO engagement is,

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:29.130
really, think about
as the overall meal

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:30.570
and not the ingredients.

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:33.780
So when your customer
comes to your restaurant,

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.270
they are really expecting
a meal to be served.

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:38.250
They're not really
interested into have

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:39.420
you got the ingredients?

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:43.380
What was the logistics behind
getting all the ingredients so

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:44.930
that you can prepare the meal?

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:48.070
Customer is really
interested in your product.

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:51.000
When you present a product,
what it should look like-- that

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:52.380
is really the focus of it.

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:55.860
And SLO helps you to
measure that exact thing--

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.300
what is the feeling
about your product--

00:13:58.300 --> 00:13:59.220
and that is the key.

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:03.390
So it's very easy to
focus on the ingredient

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:04.900
and to lose the
focus on the meal,

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:07.590
so that's the
hardest part of it.

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:10.620
Now, what helps us to bring
the focus is the mindset,

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:12.360
and you need the
whole organization

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:16.842
because if you start it just
as an engineering project,

00:14:16.842 --> 00:14:18.300
you're going to
have a lot of churn

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:20.940
because you have not brought
your other stakeholders along

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:23.607
with you who has
the say in your SLO.

00:14:23.607 --> 00:14:26.190
So that is the other thing-- you
want to focus on the mindset,

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:30.240
and frankly, in Equifax,
we started doing this,

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:33.340
and we brought Google CRE, who
is the Customer Reliability

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:35.060
Engineering crew,
helps us to execute

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:37.830
SRE best practices in Equifax.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:41.283
So that engagement
also helps us.

00:14:41.283 --> 00:14:43.700
RUXANDA DANETIU: So looking
back, this has been a journey.

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:45.860
It didn't happen overnight.

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:48.830
Is there anything that you
would have done differently

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:51.560
knowing what you know today?

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:54.550
VIPUL MAPARA: Well,
for me, personally,

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:58.930
when I took the SRE role, I
wish I knew SRE much earlier.

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:03.138
I did not know much about it, so
I had to do a lot of research.

00:15:03.138 --> 00:15:04.180
I had to read some books.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:07.060
I had to go and do a
lot of reading about it,

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:09.640
because building a
software and a product

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:12.100
that works at a global
scale is not easy.

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:15.160
You really need to have
a engineering mindset

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.980
to an operational problem,
and since my background

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:20.590
was as software engineering,
I thought, yes, I

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:22.640
think I can do SRE.

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:25.840
And the other thing I
would have appreciated,

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:29.230
I would have spent more time
learning the cloud native tools

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:32.450
and really be hands on, so I
would feel more comfortable.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:35.260
But once you get
this concept of SLO

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:38.470
and working with the
engineering mindset

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:40.870
to an operational
problem, and start

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:44.140
learning about concept of SLOs,
I think you will not look back.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:45.400
I think it's a [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:46.590
That's what I feel.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:48.340
RUXANDA DANETIU: I
know there are probably

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:50.660
a lot of people
listening in right now

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:53.920
at the beginning of
their SLO journey.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:58.180
This is a very intimidating
journey, and I want to know,

00:15:58.180 --> 00:15:59.860
can you give them some advice?

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:02.020
What do you think
is really important

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:04.410
to know at the beginning?

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:05.410
VIPUL MAPARA: Very true.

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:07.330
I mean, it is definitely
a hard journey.

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:10.140
I think what worked
for us, probably

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:13.480
I can say about a
little bit, start small.

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:16.710
Think about SLO concepts
in error budget concepts.

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:18.810
Really learn about
those concepts.

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:21.090
Decide how you want
to get started.

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:25.320
Have socialized SLO concepts
within the organization.

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:27.420
Bring your stakeholders
along with you,

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:31.380
and then figure out a small
pilot where you would really

00:16:31.380 --> 00:16:36.150
try to implement a critical
journey on an SLO around there.

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:39.190
Once you do that, I
think it helps you.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:43.530
And Google CRE helps us
to navigate stakeholders,

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:45.510
how to present the
view around it,

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:47.620
but I would start small on it.

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:52.860
Once you have that small pilot
project with a blueprint,

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:55.080
with some of the
best practices of SLO

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:57.240
and error budget
concept baked into it,

00:16:57.240 --> 00:16:59.100
the next thing
you would get into

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:00.690
is pick up the right tools.

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:03.360
You need the right tools
to achieve this scale of,

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:05.250
how do you implement SLO.

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:10.290
Frankly, I mean, in GCP,
the [INAUDIBLE] signals are

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:13.859
available out of the box,
so you could literally

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:17.099
take those [INAUDIBLE]
signals and instrument those

00:17:17.099 --> 00:17:19.619
and build your SLA out of it.

00:17:19.619 --> 00:17:23.640
And Google has a product,
Google Cloud Monitoring SLO API,

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:27.069
to allow us to build SLOs
out of those [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:30.180
So we started doing that
for the first pilot.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:33.510
It gave us a single
view for our pilot,

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:36.660
and then from that blueprint, we
started replicating and moving

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:38.310
other services to
follow the things.

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:41.490
And iteratively, we had
all of our services managed

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:44.140
through Cloud Monitoring,
and we had a single view

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:47.100
of all of our SLOs that were
important to our stakeholders.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:51.870
And it became a overall
organizational ecosystem

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:54.720
that SRE manages and
works [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:58.560
That's how I feel, if you start
small and pick the right tools

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:04.210
and have Cloud Monitoring SLO
API as your tools behind it,

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:05.123
I think it'll help.

00:18:05.123 --> 00:18:06.540
RUXANDA DANETIU:
Thank you, Vipul,

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:08.330
for sharing your personal story.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:09.460
That was really meaningful.

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:12.420
I know that there are a lot of
people listening in right now

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:14.910
at the beginning of
their SLO journey.

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:16.350
Is there any advice
that you would

00:18:16.350 --> 00:18:18.410
give somebody that's
starting that SLO

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:20.678
journey for the first time?

00:18:20.678 --> 00:18:21.720
VIPUL MAPARA: Definitely.

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:27.130
I think, at Equifax, SLO
has been a journey for us.

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:29.050
I would suggest start small.

00:18:29.050 --> 00:18:32.290
Really understand the SLO
and the error budget concept,

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:34.190
because once you
understand the concept,

00:18:34.190 --> 00:18:36.640
then you can decide
on how to get started.

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:39.190
I would advise
research internally,

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:41.170
bring the stakeholders
along with you,

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:43.810
and then identify a small pilot.

00:18:43.810 --> 00:18:47.410
Figure out the critical journeys
that goes along with the pilot,

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:49.420
and build the SLOs around them.

00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:52.180
Also, get some help
from Google CRE,

00:18:52.180 --> 00:18:55.210
who can help you to navigate the
SRE best practices on building

00:18:55.210 --> 00:18:58.990
SLOs, so that way the
blueprint that you're building

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:01.120
is reused for other services.

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:03.890
Once you have a
pilot identified,

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:08.390
and you have defined your
critical user journeys,

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:10.120
which you wanted to
define SLO around,

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:12.850
the next topic is really about
pick the right tools that

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:16.870
supports that, and
the beauty of GCP

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:18.820
is they support
[INAUDIBLE] signals out

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:20.230
of the box at Google Cloud.

00:19:20.230 --> 00:19:24.430
So that makes building the
SLO as soon as possible,

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:26.530
because you can use
[INAUDIBLE] signals

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:28.180
and start building your SLO.

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:32.610
Once you get into the SLO, you
can use Google Cloud Monitoring

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:35.200
SLO API, which allows
you to build SLOs,

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.830
and then you get all your
SLOs in a single view.

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:40.070
And I think, with
those two points,

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:44.200
I feel it's a great start.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:45.950
RUXANDA DANETIU: Thank
you so much, Vipul,

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:47.930
for taking the time
to talk to me today

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:51.410
and for sharing such a
meaningful story of your SLO

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:52.820
adoption at Equifax.

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:53.820
VIPUL MAPARA: Thank you.

00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:55.820
It's a pleasure.

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:58.530
RUXANDA DANETIU: So next,
let's take one example.

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:03.350
Let's think about SLO
monitoring for your GKE fleet.

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:05.690
And what I want us
to take a step back

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:09.650
is to understand that the SLO
monitoring functionality really

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:12.560
does not depend on the
underlying infrastructure

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:13.970
that you are deploying.

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:18.170
So within Google Cloud, we
have out of the box integration

00:20:18.170 --> 00:20:22.050
for every single
platform available.

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:24.650
So if you're using GKE
or Compute Engine or App

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:27.710
Engine or any
other GCP products,

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:31.490
you would be able to create
SLOs in that environment.

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:35.060
We have built curated,
out of the box experience

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:37.980
for the [INAUDIBLE]
this product as well.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:42.020
However, if you have a
multi-cloud deployment

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:44.720
or running on
premise environments

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:47.930
as well, if you're
bringing in your metrics

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.990
into the back end
of Cloud Monitoring,

00:20:50.990 --> 00:20:52.820
we will be able
to create and help

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:55.580
you set up your
SLOs with our API

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:58.560
very easily on those
environments as well,

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:01.730
even if they are Kubernetes
or virtual machines

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:03.800
or other clouds.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:06.950
And when we talk about
scale, SLO monitoring

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:13.370
is really build on the scale of
the entire GCP console back end

00:21:13.370 --> 00:21:15.680
for both monitoring and logging.

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:20.180
This is the same back end that
operates internal Google as

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:24.920
well, and our SLO API is
currently used internally

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:30.050
at Google, but it's also used
by our external cloud customers.

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:32.970
Everything gets built with
zero config from day one,

00:21:32.970 --> 00:21:35.360
and it gives you all
of the observability.

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:38.240
So there are a lot
of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:41.660
that Vipul mentioned and a lot
of metrics that come in out

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:44.990
of the box and get added
to the product for you

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:48.750
to be able to create
your SLOs very easily.

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:52.970
All of the functionality that
we are talking about in terms

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:58.310
of SLO monitoring at
scale, both the UI and API,

00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:01.580
they are in general
availability as of today,

00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:04.430
so we're really happy to
make this functionality

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:06.900
available to all
of our customers.

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:10.100
And in order to showcase
the UI experience,

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:12.150
Patrick Ethan, the
engineering lead

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:17.680
that have helped build the UI
and the API for SLO monitoring,

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:20.120
is going to give
us a whirlwind tour

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:24.540
of how to do SLO monitoring
for your GKE environment.

00:22:24.540 --> 00:22:27.320
Take it away, Patrick.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:28.250
PATRICK ETHAN: Hello.

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:30.020
Today, I would like
to introduce you

00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:33.380
to the service oriented
monitoring and SLO monitoring

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:37.610
features in GCP's Cloud
Monitoring product.

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:41.880
These features are currently
in general availability.

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:43.520
First, I want to
draw your attention

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:46.720
to the workspace selector
in the upper left.

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:49.460
A workspace is a
concept that allows

00:22:49.460 --> 00:22:52.970
one to combine multiple
projects into a single view.

00:22:52.970 --> 00:22:55.550
This is great for
being able to view

00:22:55.550 --> 00:23:00.620
an entire fleet of services
in a single pane of glass.

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:03.140
Next, notice that
we're in the monitoring

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:09.440
menu and the services sub
menu of the cloud console.

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:12.970
The first view you'll
see in the services area

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:17.150
is the service inventory
or services overview page.

00:23:17.150 --> 00:23:20.230
On this page, we see a list
of all of the microservices

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:25.843
that have been detected
or identified by the user.

00:23:25.843 --> 00:23:28.010
You can see that there's a
number of different types

00:23:28.010 --> 00:23:31.730
of services that depends
on the technology that's

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:34.370
used to develop those services.

00:23:34.370 --> 00:23:40.010
We currently support GAE,
Cloud Endpoints, [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:44.290
and GKE, Anthos
Service Mesh, and what

00:23:44.290 --> 00:23:48.790
we're going to focus on today,
GKE based microservices.

00:23:48.790 --> 00:23:50.630
In order to
understand these more,

00:23:50.630 --> 00:23:54.580
let's start by defining
a GKE microservice.

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:58.360
When we click Define Service,
we see that the tray slides out

00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:00.910
with a list of all
of the GKE entities

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:05.110
that are in the workspace,
including deployments, replica

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:10.420
sets, Kubernetes services,
namespaces, and so on.

00:24:10.420 --> 00:24:13.870
Depending on the model that
your organization uses,

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:17.350
any of these entities
could be a microservice.

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:22.250
Now, I know that the way
that my organization works,

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:26.600
deployments are what
correspond to microservices,

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:32.120
and I also know, from
discussions with my broader

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:37.250
organization, including
PM and eng in operations,

00:24:37.250 --> 00:24:39.080
that the ad service
is a service that

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:41.090
requires particular attention.

00:24:41.090 --> 00:24:43.070
So we search for ad
service and find out

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:46.340
there's actually two of them
running in our workspace.

00:24:46.340 --> 00:24:52.400
We'll select one of them, give
them a name, click Submit,

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:58.810
and with that, we've created the
first GKE based microservice.

00:24:58.810 --> 00:25:01.600
Next, you can read more
about best practices

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:05.950
for monitoring services at
the documentation link here,

00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:08.530
or you can move directly
to creating a service level

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:11.430
objective.

00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:15.300
Instead, we're going to
go back to the inventory

00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:18.000
and notice that our new
microservice appears

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.070
in the inventory already.

00:25:20.070 --> 00:25:22.770
It includes the
metadata labels that

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:25.650
are used to define
the microservice,

00:25:25.650 --> 00:25:30.450
including the cluster name,
CSM demo, and the deployment ad

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:31.600
service.

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:34.530
It also indicates that we
don't have any SLOs or SLO

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:36.450
based alerts yet.

00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:43.130
If we click into the dashboard
for the service itself,

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:47.580
at the top of that, we see that
the first piece of monitoring

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:51.930
data is about SLOs.

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:54.360
This is granted space
at the top of the page

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:57.960
because SLOs are one of the most
valuable things for monitoring

00:25:57.960 --> 00:25:59.170
services.

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.050
Now, we don't yet
have any silos,

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:04.740
so let's fix that
by creating an SLO.

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:06.390
Again, this
describes the service

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:10.230
that we're creating
the SLO alert for.

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:15.030
Some service types have
default availability or latency

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:17.400
metrics, but we're going
to pick our own metric.

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:21.530
Now, there are a
number of metrics

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:24.770
that are available out
of the box for all users

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:26.570
to define SLOs.

00:26:26.570 --> 00:26:29.390
For example, here is a
list of container metrics

00:26:29.390 --> 00:26:32.550
that may be useful
for defining SLOs.

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:37.280
GCLBs also have a number of
metrics which can be useful.

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:40.430
As the developer of
this app, however, I

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:43.490
know that the thing
that best reflects

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:47.240
the performance
of the ad service

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:50.720
is the response latency metric.

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:54.710
Based on discussions
with PM and others,

00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:56.540
I know that customers
are satisfied

00:26:56.540 --> 00:26:59.480
with the ad service when it
responds in 30 milliseconds

00:26:59.480 --> 00:27:06.140
or less on any of the containers
that are running that image.

00:27:06.140 --> 00:27:08.630
Once we describe the
metric that we're

00:27:08.630 --> 00:27:10.750
going to use to
evaluate the SLO,

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:16.010
we can see a preview of how that
metric is actually performing,

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:19.280
and we can also guess that
30 milliseconds is actually

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:22.490
a pretty aggressive target
given the behavior of the system

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:24.110
right now.

00:27:24.110 --> 00:27:27.440
To finish defining the SLO,
we set the compliance period

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:32.600
to be a calendar week and
a performance goal of 90%.

00:27:32.600 --> 00:27:37.130
What that means is that 90% of
all requests to the ad service

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:38.960
need to finish in
30 milliseconds

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:41.750
in order to be compliant.

00:27:41.750 --> 00:27:45.670
Finally, we give it a
name, we click Submit,

00:27:45.670 --> 00:27:47.895
and now we've created
our first SLO.

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:56.730
We can evaluate the SLO based
on the historical behavior

00:27:56.730 --> 00:27:58.410
of the system, so we
don't have to wait

00:27:58.410 --> 00:28:00.540
for history to accumulate.

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:03.300
We can see that, right now,
the service level indicator,

00:28:03.300 --> 00:28:06.030
the metric, is
running at about 95%,

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:08.980
which is greater
than our 90% target.

00:28:08.980 --> 00:28:11.700
We can also see that about
half of the error budget

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:14.450
is remaining for the week.

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:22.350
If we expand the time
selector out to a week,

00:28:22.350 --> 00:28:25.680
we can see that, indeed, at
the beginning of the week,

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:28.905
the error budget resets
and then trails off

00:28:28.905 --> 00:28:30.780
as the week progresses.

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:33.180
Based on last week, it
looks like we will run out

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:35.880
of error budget,
so there's probably

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:39.540
some reliability work that needs
to be done for this service.

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:42.120
Notice, also, that
there is a view

00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:46.950
to look at SLO based alerts that
are firing for this service.

00:28:46.950 --> 00:28:50.010
In order to create these,
you can use this link here,

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:53.160
and that alert can be
created right in context.

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:55.950
The SLO based alerts
are based on the concept

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:58.860
of a fast burn alert, which
means that the system can

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:02.260
detect when the error budget is
being burned at a rate that's

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:06.720
not sustainable, and such
that the system can remain

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:09.100
in compliance.

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:12.550
If we look at other parts
of the service dashboard,

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:15.550
we can see that the metrics
widget is interesting.

00:29:15.550 --> 00:29:18.520
It contains a lot of the
out of the box metrics

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:20.830
that are available
to users arranged

00:29:20.830 --> 00:29:22.680
in a number of bundles.

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:24.850
It's currently seeing
the container metrics

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:27.130
corresponding to CPU.

00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:29.380
We can also look
at, for example,

00:29:29.380 --> 00:29:33.250
pod level metrics
on network usage.

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:37.400
We select that, and
the charts update.

00:29:37.400 --> 00:29:42.330
We can see individual pods and
the network usage on those.

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:47.760
Alternatively, we can look
at a node metric such as CPU

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:49.980
to see what kind of
capacity our cluster has.

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:57.500
If we scroll down further, we
can see the Kubernetes pods

00:29:57.500 --> 00:29:59.690
that make up this service.

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:03.590
Here, we see we have five pods
running the ad service right

00:30:03.590 --> 00:30:04.340
now.

00:30:04.340 --> 00:30:07.580
All of them are ready and
running, so they're healthy.

00:30:07.580 --> 00:30:10.750
For each of them, resource
utilization is described,

00:30:10.750 --> 00:30:12.500
and under the
snowman, we see a link

00:30:12.500 --> 00:30:15.300
to the Kubernetes engine page.

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:19.772
If we switch to the Kubernetes
part of the cloud console,

00:30:19.772 --> 00:30:21.480
this is where we can
see more information

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:25.140
about the pod, including
its events, its logs,

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:27.900
and also take actions on the
pod such as restarting it.

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:37.580
The last part of the service
dashboard is the logs widget.

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:40.270
This widget gives
a brief overview

00:30:40.270 --> 00:30:43.060
of the logs that are being
emitted by the microservice.

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:46.030
If, however, we want to look
at the logs in more detail,

00:30:46.030 --> 00:30:50.720
we can go to the logs viewer.

00:30:50.720 --> 00:30:53.570
Now, the interesting thing
about pivoting to the log viewer

00:30:53.570 --> 00:30:56.630
is that the context,
or the filter state,

00:30:56.630 --> 00:30:59.450
for the microservice
is preserved.

00:30:59.450 --> 00:31:02.060
You can see here that
the filter, by default,

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:06.320
goes to the cluster that we've
been monitoring and the ad

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:07.400
service.

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:10.970
Even the timestamp
filter is brought across

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:12.770
to the logs viewer
to preserve context.

00:31:15.710 --> 00:31:19.330
The logs viewer shows a
histogram of log volume.

00:31:19.330 --> 00:31:23.430
It also facets it by severity.

00:31:23.430 --> 00:31:27.450
We can filter to errors,
to the error severity,

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:31.440
and very quickly get to the
stack traces corresponding

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:32.640
to our microservice.

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:40.470
Now, let's return to the
idea of SLO based alerts.

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:42.480
If we go to the
overview, we can see

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:45.960
that there are three SLO based
alerts that are currently

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:47.740
firing.

00:31:47.740 --> 00:31:50.400
If we filter to those services,
we see that one of them

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:54.230
is the default GKE namespace.

00:31:54.230 --> 00:31:57.020
Let's look at what a service
level dashboard looks like when

00:31:57.020 --> 00:31:59.090
there are incidents firing.

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:00.890
The thing you'll
notice different

00:32:00.890 --> 00:32:05.780
is that now there is an
alert timeline at the top.

00:32:05.780 --> 00:32:07.970
The timeline indicates
when incidents

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:11.210
are firing by the red
bars on the timeline.

00:32:11.210 --> 00:32:14.030
If you hover on these
bars, you see a summary

00:32:14.030 --> 00:32:18.658
of the corresponding
alert along with a link

00:32:18.658 --> 00:32:19.700
to get the alert details.

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:33.310
The alert timeline can also
be used as a time selector,

00:32:33.310 --> 00:32:36.130
and when you select
the time there,

00:32:36.130 --> 00:32:40.070
that also updates the
time for all the charts

00:32:40.070 --> 00:32:44.020
and for all the log entries.

00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:49.960
You should also notice that
this default microservice has

00:32:49.960 --> 00:32:52.960
two SLOs defined, so you
can have more than one SLO

00:32:52.960 --> 00:32:54.890
defined for a single service.

00:32:54.890 --> 00:32:58.420
You can also have more than one
alert defined for a single SLO.

00:33:01.380 --> 00:33:05.250
That completes a tour of the UI
for service oriented monitoring

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:07.310
and SLOs.

00:33:07.310 --> 00:33:10.200
You should note that if
you're interested in managing

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:12.750
large numbers of
services or SLOs,

00:33:12.750 --> 00:33:15.780
there is an API that's also
available that allows access

00:33:15.780 --> 00:33:17.890
to all of these features.

00:33:17.890 --> 00:33:19.420
It has been my
pleasure to introduce

00:33:19.420 --> 00:33:22.150
you to service oriented
monitoring concepts today

00:33:22.150 --> 00:33:24.070
in Cloud Monitoring.

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:25.177
Thank you.

